     "text": "```python\nfrom typing import Union\n\ndef int_to_roman(num: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts an integer to a Roman numeral.\n\n    The function uses a list of value-symbol pairs, where each pair represents\n    a value and its corresponding Roman numeral symbol. The algorithm iterates\n    through the list, subtracting the largest possible value from the input number\n    and appending the corresponding symbol to the result string until the number\n    is reduced to zero.\n\n    Parameters:\n    num (int): The integer to be converted to a Roman numeral.\n\n    Returns:\n    str: The Roman numeral representation of the input integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    val_sym = [\n        (1000, 'M'),\n        (900, 'CM'),\n        (500, 'D'),\n        (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'),\n        (90, 'XC'),\n        (50, 'L'),\n        (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'),\n        (9, 'IX'),\n        (5, 'V'),\n        (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    roman_num = ''\n    for value, symbol in val_sym:\n        while num >= value:\n            roman_num += symbol\n            num -= value\n        if num == 0:\n            break\n    return roman_num\n```",
